WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.188
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online TOEFL preparation.

2
00:00:05.488 --> 00:00:09.141
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.441 --> 00:00:17.864
Unit three: Reading: Read in Daily Life. Today you will find facts and make inferences from emails, notices and online posts.

4
00:00:18.164 --> 00:00:20.851
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.137
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.983 --> 00:00:26.950
Notice.

7
00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:30.159
Notice means a short written announcement.

8
00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:32.982
The notice says the library is closed.

9
00:00:33.583 --> 00:00:36.154
Your turn. Say: Notice.

10
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:39.966
Notice.

11
00:00:40.717 --> 00:00:41.544
Email.

12
00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:44.735
Email means a message sent online.

13
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:46.990
Read the email carefully.

14
00:00:47.583 --> 00:00:50.003
Your turn. Say: Email.

15
00:00:52.867 --> 00:00:53.810
Email.

16
00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:55.481
Infer.

17
00:00:56.483 --> 00:00:59.934
Infer means to understand something not directly stated.

18
00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:02.666
You can infer the writer is unhappy.

19
00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:05.600
Your turn. Say: Infer.

20
00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:09.294
Infer.

21
00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:10.947
Purpose.

22
00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:14.333
Purpose means the reason something is written.

23
00:01:14.917 --> 00:01:16.893
The purpose is to invite people.

24
00:01:17.483 --> 00:01:19.811
Your turn. Say: Purpose.

25
00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:23.645
Purpose.

26
00:01:24.383 --> 00:01:25.303
Deadline.

27
00:01:26.317 --> 00:01:28.583
Deadline means the last date to do something.

28
00:01:29.183 --> 00:01:30.695
The deadline is Friday.

29
00:01:31.283 --> 00:01:33.947
Your turn. Say: Deadline.

30
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:37.720
Deadline.

31
00:01:38.483 --> 00:01:39.415
Detail.

32
00:01:40.417 --> 00:01:43.554
Detail means a small piece of specific information.

33
00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:45.917
Find the detail about the room.

34
00:01:46.517 --> 00:01:49.030
Your turn. Say: Detail.

35
00:01:51.867 --> 00:01:52.972
Detail.

36
00:01:53.717 --> 00:01:54.648
Reply.

37
00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:57.684
Reply means to answer a message.

38
00:01:58.283 --> 00:01:59.842
Reply by Monday.

39
00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.335
Your turn. Say: Reply.

40
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.248
Reply.

41
00:02:07.983 --> 00:02:08.845
Schedule.

42
00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:11.942
Schedule means a plan of times.

43
00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:13.807
Check the schedule.

44
00:02:14.417 --> 00:02:16.999
Your turn. Say: Schedule.

45
00:02:19.833 --> 00:02:20.916
Schedule.

46
00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:25.583
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:26.417 --> 00:02:29.357
Visal, what texts appear in Read in Daily Life?

48
00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:44.062
You read everyday texts such as emails, notices, social media posts and short articles. The questions ask for facts, the purpose of the text and what you can infer.

49
00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.045
How do I find a fact quickly?

50
00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:57.583
Read the question first, then scan the text for keywords like dates, names and places. Read the sentences around the keyword to be sure.

51
00:02:58.683 --> 00:03:00.277
What is an inference?

52
00:03:01.383 --> 00:03:12.615
An inference is something that the text suggests but does not say directly. For example, if a notice says the room is full again, you can infer that the event is popular.

53
00:03:13.767 --> 00:03:15.580
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:16.417 --> 00:03:20.645
Grammar focus: Strategy: purpose, detail, inference.

55
00:03:20.945 --> 00:03:22.213
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:22.917 --> 00:03:26.518
The notice says the deadline is Friday, so I must reply before then.

57
00:03:27.417 --> 00:03:30.844
The room is full again, so the event is probably popular.

58
00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:39.070
Identify the purpose of the text, scan for details with keywords, and use clues to infer ideas that are not stated directly.

59
00:03:39.817 --> 00:03:44.164
Be careful! Don't say: I use my own knowledge for facts.

60
00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:48.854
Say: I find the fact in the text.

61
00:03:49.154 --> 00:03:50.655
Answer from the text.

62
00:03:51.467 --> 00:03:54.154
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:54.967 --> 00:03:56.316
A Campus Notice.

64
00:03:56.616 --> 00:04:00.682
A notice tells students that the language club will move to Room 12.

65
00:04:01.667 --> 00:04:04.421
It says the club meets every Tuesday at four.

66
00:04:05.433 --> 00:04:07.828
It asks students to reply by Friday.

67
00:04:08.833 --> 00:04:11.170
It also says the old room was too small.

68
00:04:12.167 --> 00:04:14.828
Students can infer that the club is popular.

69
00:04:15.933 --> 00:04:19.656
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:20.517 --> 00:04:22.516
The purpose of a text is blank.

71
00:04:22.816 --> 00:04:27.085
A, why it was written. B, its colour. C, its length only.

72
00:04:30.633 --> 00:04:33.062
The answer is A: why it was written.

73
00:04:34.217 --> 00:04:36.274
To infer is to understand blank.

74
00:04:36.574 --> 00:04:41.389
A, spelling. B, what is not stated directly. C, only numbers.

75
00:04:44.933 --> 00:04:47.873
The answer is B: what is not stated directly.

76
00:04:49.017 --> 00:04:50.494
What is the deadline?

77
00:04:50.794 --> 00:04:54.238
A, Tuesday. B, Monday. C, Friday.

78
00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:59.718
The answer is C: Friday.

79
00:05:00.883 --> 00:05:03.626
Why can students infer the club is popular?

80
00:05:03.926 --> 00:05:09.135
A, the old room was too small. B, it is on Tuesday. C, it is free.

81
00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:15.953
The answer is A: the old room was too small.

82
00:05:17.117 --> 00:05:18.617
Where does the club meet now?

83
00:05:18.917 --> 00:05:22.919
A, the library. B, Room 12. C, Room 2.

84
00:05:26.467 --> 00:05:28.582
The answer is B: Room 12.

85
00:05:29.767 --> 00:05:32.802
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:36.694
How do you answer questions about an email or notice?

87
00:05:45.483 --> 00:05:47.460
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:47.760 --> 00:06:01.653
I read the question first and then find the purpose of the text. I scan for keywords such as dates and names, and I read the sentences around them. If the answer is not stated, I use clues in the text to make an inference.

89
00:06:01.953 --> 00:06:03.488
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:06:04.383 --> 00:06:10.658
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and strategy: purpose, detail, inference.

91
00:06:10.958 --> 00:06:15.770
Today's value is critical thinking. Think carefully and give reasons for your opinions.

92
00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:18.571
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

93
00:06:18.871 --> 00:06:22.313
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

